From ea737715e1c19a1ee2291dc82b1bc9e6ead8659a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:45:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] blitz-dom: an inline svg is drawn with the styles the
 document gives it

An inline svg is drawn from one usvg parse of its markup, and usvg reads
the markup alone: none of the document's rules, no custom property. A
shape filled by `.icon rect { fill: ... }`, or by var() in a rule or in
its own style, drew black.

The markup usvg is given (Node::svg_source) now carries, in each
element's style attribute, the painting properties the cascade computed
for it: fill and stroke (a colour as rgb(), a paint server as url(#id)),
their opacities, stroke width, caps, joins, miter limit, dashes, and a
nested element's opacity. A property is written where the element sets
it (by attribute or style) or where it differs from its parent's, so a
<use> still passes its values to a symbol's shapes that set none. The
svg's own opacity is its box's, which Blitz paints: usvg is told not to
apply it again. The hash an svg is rebuilt by (inline_svg_changed) is
taken of this markup, so a theme that changes a custom property redraws
its icons.

For that cascade to be a browser's, an SVG element's presentation
attributes (fill, stroke, their opacities, stroke-width and the other
stroke properties, opacity, color) are now presentational hints, parsed
when they change (ElementData::presentation_attributes): below every
author rule. Stylo did not see them before, and a rule could not
override them in usvg either. A value with var() is dropped, as
browsers drop it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
